gm-method debugging lesson: a 'X appears after a long time / X not there' complaint is often NOT a render/logic bug but a slow INITIAL BUILD that blocks time-to-availability. Witness via the SERVER/build LOG (timestamps), not just the page - a log line like 'built 9781ms' on a time-budget-CHUNKED build means the work doesn't freeze the loop but the RESULT still doesn't EXIST for ~10s. Profile the cost as count x per-unit (e.g. N^2 grid samples x per-sample ms) to confirm it's count-bound, then the fix is COARSE-FIRST: build a low-res/cheap version fast (1/16th the work), install it so the thing is AVAILABLE immediately, then build the full-res version in the background and atomic-swap. Cuts time-to-availability ~20x with no permanent fidelity loss. Also: when a user reports two symptoms together ('X missing AND Y broken'), AskUserQuestion to pin the exact repro - they often share ONE root (here a slow load made both terrain AND the player VRM appear late) rather than being two bugs. When the managed browser daemon crashes repeatedly (UV_HANDLE_CLOSING) on a diagnostic-only client global you added, REVERT the diagnostic expose rather than fighting the witness gate - the real fix may be in a server file (no client-edit gate), and the server log is the authoritative witness for server-side behavior.